// HOW AN INSTALLED PART IS IDENTIFIED, for any board read at the bench. A part credits its
// datasheet row (extdeps.electronic_component) only once its marking is read and matched; until then it
// contributes no property. The vocabulary was first written for the P4261 carriers (product.p4261) and
// holds nothing particular to them.

// ── IDENTIFICATION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   IdentifiedByMarking      the marking matches one maker's table, so the part's specification is cited
//   MarkingSharedByMakers    the marking matches one code in several makers' tables and the maker is not
//                            identified: a property counts only where every candidate supports it
//   MarkingCandidate         the marking matches one maker's code, but whether that maker made the part
//                            is unread: a lead, contributing no property until the discriminator is read
//   RatingPrintedOnBody      the part prints its own rating; no datasheet, and no decoding either
//   ResistanceCodeRead       a resistance code, read and decoded
//   MarkingUnresolved        the marking is read (or unread) and NOT decoded; it contributes nothing
//   FootprintUnpopulated     a footprint with no part on it
//
// Each reading of a marking carries its own receipt, so a part never inherits another's identity by
// sitting nearby or looking alike.

// THE MARKING TEXT, AND WHAT WAS SAID BESIDE IT (marking contract §1, revision 5).
//
// WHY A NAME FOR THE TEXT RATHER THAN A PLAIN STRING. The rule it carries is the one the contract calls
// the only one worth a type: a marking row with no glyphs is not a reading, and it cannot be written.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO PREDICATE ON IT. The draft refused a span sitting in brackets, on the premise that
// brackets occur only in the bench's editorial asides. The corpus falsified the premise: U451's printed
// text includes "(swirl logo)" and "(e4)", and U10CX085's includes "(logo)" and "(circled)". What is
// printed on a body and what the bench said beside it are written with the SAME characters, so no
// predicate over characters can sort them, and a type that claimed to would only be a guess with a
// compiler's authority behind it. The guard moved to a control that does not ask a string what it means:
// `duplicate_channel_claim` refuses a row that records one observed span in two channels at once.
// THE MARKING, AS OBSERVATION (marking contract §6, revision 6 — approved 2026-11-05).
//
// WHY A SPAN IS THE UNIT. What the bench records about one body is several things at once: characters
// printed on it, a maker's logo, a physical feature, and something said about the reading itself. Rev 5
// kept the first in `text` and the rest in a side list, which forced every check to guess where one
// channel ended — and the guess was a word list matched against prose, which §1 forbids and which the
// corpus defeated twice (printed text really does contain "(logo)" and "(swirl)"). A span carries its
// own classification, so the channels are separated by the TYPE, and nothing inspects words to decide.
//
// WHY THE HEAD OF A TRANSCRIPTION IS A GLYPH SPAN, NOT A LIST. The contract writes
// `Transcribed { first: MarkingSpan.Glyphs, rest }`: a reading that recorded only annotations would be a
// no-glyph read wearing the clothes of a transcription. This language has no way to name one arm of a sum
// as a type, so the glyphs arm is factored out as `GlyphSpan` and used BOTH inside the `Glyphs` arm and as
// the head of `Transcribed` — the same guarantee, spelled structurally: `Transcribed { [BodyFeature] }`
// cannot be built. `LookedNothingReadable` is the other arm, and it is the honest home for "looked, read
// no glyphs", which rev 5 could only express as the string "none" and then guess at.
//
// WHY AN UNREAD MARK IS NOT A DISPUTE. '?' means "this glyph is not readable": silence, not dissent. It
// consumes no dispute and grounds no identification; it does still record where the read came from. The
// corpus is why this is settled rather than argued: U12 reads `P617A / 01 14 / 226` and U13, the same
// part type, reads `P617A / ?? ?? / ???`; Q3_5 reads `BC4X` beside `BC4X?`; `WEJ` beside `?WEJ` beside
// `?W?J`. Treating '?' as disagreement would manufacture conflicts between identical parts, and one such
// pair sits on a row that relies on its identification today.

// Characters printed on the body. The name is the contract's only rule worth a type: a reading with no
// glyphs is not a transcription, and it cannot be written.
type MarkingGlyphs = NonEmptyStr

// ONE OBSERVED SPAN AND THE PIXELS IT WAS READ FROM. `sighting` is the frame and the box, bound to the
// span rather than to the reading: an identification may rest on a span only when that span's own
// evidence can be checked (§6.3). Absent is legal and is recorded evidence debt — such a span may sit in
// `others`, and it can never carry reliance.
type GlyphSpan {
  preceded_by: String
  span: MarkingGlyphs
  sighting: PhotographCitation?
}

// A span, classified by what it is, at the point it is recorded. The duplicate-channel control reads this
// classification and nothing else: two spans of DIFFERENT kinds claiming the SAME digest and the SAME box
// is the contradiction, so the control asks about evidence identity — never about text, and never about
// boxes overlapping.
type MarkingAnnotation
  = PrintedLogo { preceded_by: String, span: NonEmptyStr, whose: NonEmptyStr, sighting: PhotographCitation? }
  | BodyFeature { preceded_by: String, span: NonEmptyStr, described: NonEmptyStr, sighting: PhotographCitation? }
  | ReadingCircumstance { preceded_by: String, span: NonEmptyStr, noted: NonEmptyStr, sighting: PhotographCitation? }

type MarkingSpan
  = Glyphs { glyphs: GlyphSpan }
  | Annotation { annotation: MarkingAnnotation }

// What one bench session recorded about one marking. Two states, and the difference between them is the
// whole point: glyphs were transcribed, or the bench looked and could not read any.
type MarkingContent
  = Transcribed { leading: List<MarkingAnnotation>, first: GlyphSpan, rest: List<MarkingSpan>, followed_by: String }
  | LookedNothingReadable { circumstances: List<MarkingAnnotation>, followed_by: String }

// One bench reading of one marking. `content` carries the spans; `evidence` is the receipt for the reading
// as a whole, and is what a `MarkingObservation` binds its reliance to.
type MarkingReading {
  content: MarkingContent
  evidence: EvidenceRef
}

// THE ONE REDUCTION THE CONTRACT MANDATES (§3): transcribed lines split at "/", with the declared
// non-glyph spacing removed from each. WHY ONE PLACE AND NOT TWO NEIGHBOURING IMPLEMENTATIONS: the rule
// "spaces inside a transcribed line are not glyph identity" was carried in a witness comment while the
// model compared raw strings, and the two disagreed in front of a reviewer. Any comparison that needs the
// reduction calls THESE functions, so changing the reduction changes one thing and a witness notices.
//
// WHY THE REDUCTION IS A CHARACTER LIST AND NOT TEXT. Host text and a structural List<Char> are different
// values in this language: string_eq refuses a cons-built list ("chars expects a string argument, got
// List", measured), so text is never reassembled from scalars -- reports fold it back with
// unicode_scalar_fold, the conversion std.coercion names -- and every position that gets COMPARED is
// compared as a Char. Separators are never written as literals either: v2.std.text has no character
// literals, so the separator and the spacing scalar come out of one-character host strings via string_head.
//
// WHY AN UNAVAILABLE REDUCTION IS A DISPUTE AND NOT A SHRUG. If a scalar cannot be obtained, no comparison
// can be made; reporting nothing would let no_dissent mint over a comparison that never happened, which is
// the one thing this function exists to prevent. So the failure is itself a reported dispute, and the only
// standing it can produce is a preferred-under-dissent one -- which requires the relied-on reading to be
// checkable for its subject, so a reduction that cannot run cannot smuggle an identification through.

type ReducedLine {
  glyphs: List<Char>
}

type GlyphReduction
  = Reduced { lines: List<ReducedLine> }
  | ReductionUnavailable { why: NonEmptyStr }

// The text of a transcribed string, for COMPARISON only: identity never goes through text, and the prose
// that leaves this module is built by std's own fold.
//
// WHY THIS HELPER'S RESULT IS ALWAYS BOUND TO A NAME BEFORE IT IS PASSED.
//
// A seed defect substitutes an ARGUMENT'S SOURCE TEXT into the callee's parameter list. It is invisible to
// tokenize, parse, resolve and typecheck and dies at eval (about step 88) with, for example:
//
//   call contract mismatch calling 'unicode_scalar_unfold': no parameter named 'host' (declared: [s])
//
// where the name is whatever source text stood in the caller's argument position -- so the word moves when
// the argument is renamed. 'unicode_scalar_unfold' (src/v2/std/integer.dag:1119, one parameter `s`) is
// simply the first inner call that checks its labels, so it is where every shape surfaces.
//
// Measured, each row a separate claim_batch run (the shapes, by name; the probe sources are not part of this tree):
//   PASS  non-recursive, literal argument                           into string_any_char_in
//   PASS  non-recursive, bound name (coerced) + literal alphabet   into string_any_char_in   (f7)
//   PASS  non-recursive, bound name + destructured Char             into string_contains_char (f2, f9)
//   PASS  non-recursive, call expression as the argument            into string_eq             (f1)
//   FAIL  non-recursive, bound name in the `alphabet:` label         into string_any_char_in   (f3)
//   FAIL  RECURSIVE,   bound name                                    into string_contains_char (f10)
//   FAIL  RECURSIVE,   bound name (coerced)                          into string_contains_char (f8)
//   FAIL  RECURSIVE,   bound name (coerced)                          into string_eq             (f11)
//
// So the two shapes this module keeps away from these calls are: a bound name reached from a RECURSIVE frame,
// and a bound name in `alphabet:`. Recursion is the switch, not the callee -- f8, f10 and f11 are the same
// function handed three different predicates, and the same function without the recursive arm (f9) is green.
// The smallest repro is a twelve-line recursive function handing a bound name to string_contains_char (f8).
//
// It is NOT a cast, NOT an identifier spelled like the keyword `as`, and NOT a member access: each of those
// was eliminated by measured shapes (five, seven and one respectively, kept with this work's receipts in the
// private atlas branch), and `reduced_marking` below has carried a cast inside a labelled argument for
// months. Renaming this helper moved the word inside the error message and changed nothing else.
//
// What this note does NOT claim: that the boundary above is complete. It is drawn where it was measured.
// `span_carries_mark` below exists because of this defect -- it is the leaf the recursive walk is not
// allowed to be -- and an earlier revision of this comment justified these bindings with probe rows that
// never called the failing function at all.
//
// The defect is filed: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_generated_crossing_call_is_labelled_from_source_text_not_the_callee_parameter.
// These bindings are REQUIRED at the recursive-frame sites that call this helper, for v1-interpreted evaluation, because of that row (the non-recursive sites span_carries_mark and scalar_of cast inline: a run without their bindings passed); a v1 fix
// was judged not admissible under v1_maintenance_standing (no v2 self-host caller exists). Restoration trigger,
// the row's own: a v1 fix, or witness evaluation moving to the native compiler; then these bindings go.
fn shown_text(t: NonEmptyStr) -> String {
  t as String
}

fn scalar_of(one: NonEmptyStr) -> Char? {
  match string_head(s: one as String) {
    CharFound { value: c } => Present { value: c }
    CharAbsent => Absent
  }
}

// Text back out of characters, for REPORTS only. Comparison never goes this way: host text and a
// structural list of scalars are different values here (string_eq refuses a cons-built list with "chars
// expects a string argument, got List", measured), so what gets compared is always a Char, and the only
// text that leaves this module is a report's prose built by std's own fold.
fn text_of(glyphs: List<Char>) -> String {
  unicode_scalar_fold(xs: glyphs)
}

fn reduced_marking(text: MarkingGlyphs) -> GlyphReduction {
  // The host text is bound once, on a line of its own, so the two unfold calls below read as work on one
  // string. NOT because a cast cannot sit inside an argument list: it can, five shapes of it measured in
  // a probe run on 2026-11-05, including this exact one (the probe is not kept in this tree). What cannot
  // be done is NAME a parameter or pattern `as` -- the first version of `same_chars` declared
  // `(as: List<Char>, bs: ...)` and every call to it died at RUNTIME with "calling 'unicode_scalar_unfold':
  // no parameter named 'as' (declared: [s])", because the labelled argument `as:` collides with the cast
  // keyword. The compiler is silent about it where it is written; that is the defect to report, not the
  // cast. Renaming the parameters to l:/r: is what fixed it.
  let host = text as String
  match scalar_of(one: "/") {
    Absent => ReductionUnavailable { why: "no scalar could be obtained for the line separator" }
    Present { value: separator } =>
      match scalar_of(one: " ") {
        Absent => ReductionUnavailable { why: "no scalar could be obtained for transcribed spacing" }
        Present { value: spacing } =>
          Reduced {
            lines: collect_reduced_lines(
              rest: unicode_scalar_unfold(s: host),
              separator: separator,
              spacing: spacing,
              current: [],
              done: []
            )
          }
      }
  }
}

// Splitting at the separator rather than at the three-character " / " is the SAME reduction, because the
// spaces around a line break are spacing and the rule that drops the spaces inside a line drops them too.
// The witness that reduces a real multi-line transcription is what would notice if that were untrue.
fn collect_reduced_lines(
  rest: List<Char>,
  separator: Char,
  spacing: Char,
  current: List<Char>,
  done: List<ReducedLine>
) -> List<ReducedLine> {
  match rest {
    Empty => list_push(xs: done, item: ReducedLine { glyphs: current })
    Cons { head: c, tail: fewer } =>
      if char_eq(a: c, b: separator) {
        collect_reduced_lines(
          rest: fewer,
          separator: separator,
          spacing: spacing,
          current: [],
          done: list_push(xs: done, item: ReducedLine { glyphs: current })
        )
      } else if char_eq(a: c, b: spacing) {
        collect_reduced_lines(
          rest: fewer, separator: separator, spacing: spacing, current: current, done: done
        )
      } else {
        collect_reduced_lines(
          rest: fewer,
          separator: separator,
          spacing: spacing,
          current: list_push(xs: current, item: c),
          done: done
        )
      }
  }
}

// The two characters at one position, as a report names them: the relied-on glyph versus the one another
// reading saw there. The word is a report and nothing branches on it, which is why it carries no type of
// its own; what is typed is the list's membership, which is what every standing decision reads.
fn disputed_pair(left: String, right: String) -> NonEmptyStr {
  string_join(fields: [left, " vs ", right], separator: "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn char_text(c: Char) -> String {
  text_of(glyphs: [c])
}

fn missing_text() -> String {
  "(no character)"
}

// WHERE TWO REDUCED LINES DISAGREE, position for position. Two readings of different length dissent at
// every position the longer one has beyond the shorter: the shorter one read nothing there, and "nothing"
// is a position, not an absence of disagreement. Characters are compared here and never whole strings, so
// the reduction above decides what counts as the same glyph and nothing else can quietly re-add spacing.
fn positions_in_dispute(relied_glyphs: List<Char>, other_glyphs: List<Char>) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match relied_glyphs {
    Empty => other_glyphs.map(ch => disputed_pair(left: missing_text(), right: char_text(ch)))
    Cons { head: c, tail: fewer } =>
      match other_glyphs {
        Empty => relied_glyphs.map(ch => disputed_pair(left: char_text(ch), right: missing_text()))
        Cons { head: d, tail: fewer_d } =>
          if char_eq(a: c, b: d) {
            positions_in_dispute(relied_glyphs: fewer, other_glyphs: fewer_d)
          } else {
            [disputed_pair(left: char_text(c), right: char_text(d))]
              + positions_in_dispute(relied_glyphs: fewer, other_glyphs: fewer_d)
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── SPANS: THE UNIT OF A RECORDING (contract §6.2) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A span knows which channel it was recorded in, so every control below reads a classification instead of
// asking prose what it means. §1 forbids the alternative — "or any word list" — and the corpus proved the
// point twice: what is printed on a body really does include "(logo)" and "(swirl)".

// What was observed, quoted. Each arm's companion field says what it MEANS, and the companion is never what
// a control compares: comparing descriptions is how two channels both containing "TI" once looked like
// duplication, when the contradiction is one observed span recorded in two channels.
fn annotation_quoted(a: MarkingAnnotation) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match a {
    PrintedLogo { preceded_by: _, span, whose: _, sighting: _ } => span
    BodyFeature { preceded_by: _, span, described: _, sighting: _ } => span
    ReadingCircumstance { preceded_by: _, span, noted: _, sighting: _ } => span
  }
}

fn span_quoted(s: MarkingSpan) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match s {
    Glyphs { glyphs } => glyphs.span as NonEmptyStr
    Annotation { annotation } => annotation_quoted(a: annotation)
  }
}

// The pixels this span was read from, if any. Absent is RECORDED EVIDENCE DEBT, not a claim about the whole
// frame: such a span may sit among `others`, and can never carry reliance (§6.3).
fn annotation_sighting(a: MarkingAnnotation) -> PhotographCitation? {
  match a {
    PrintedLogo { preceded_by: _, span: _, whose: _, sighting } => sighting
    BodyFeature { preceded_by: _, span: _, described: _, sighting } => sighting
    ReadingCircumstance { preceded_by: _, span: _, noted: _, sighting } => sighting
  }
}

fn span_sighting(s: MarkingSpan) -> PhotographCitation? {
  match s {
    Glyphs { glyphs } => glyphs.sighting
    Annotation { annotation } => annotation_sighting(a: annotation)
  }
}

// Which channel a span was recorded in, as data. Four, closed: glyphs, a maker's printed logo, a physical
// feature of the package, a circumstance of the reading.
fn annotation_channel(a: MarkingAnnotation) -> Nat {
  match a {
    PrintedLogo { preceded_by: _, span: _, whose: _, sighting: _ } => 1
    BodyFeature { preceded_by: _, span: _, described: _, sighting: _ } => 2
    ReadingCircumstance { preceded_by: _, span: _, noted: _, sighting: _ } => 3
  }
}

fn span_channel(s: MarkingSpan) -> Nat {
  match s {
    Glyphs { glyphs: _ } => 0
    Annotation { annotation } => annotation_channel(a: annotation)
  }
}

fn channels_differ(a: MarkingSpan, b: MarkingSpan) -> Bool {
  match nat_compare(a: span_channel(s: a), b: span_channel(s: b)) {
    Equal => false
    Less => true
    Greater => true
  }
}

// Every span a reading recorded, in the order the bench wrote them.
fn content_spans(c: MarkingContent) -> List<MarkingSpan> {
  match c {
    Transcribed { leading, first, rest, followed_by: _ } =>
      map(leading, a => Annotation { annotation: a })
        + Cons { head: Glyphs { glyphs: first }, tail: rest }
    LookedNothingReadable { circumstances, followed_by: _ } =>
      map(circumstances, a => Annotation { annotation: a })
  }
}

fn glyph_spans_in(spans: List<MarkingSpan>) -> List<GlyphSpan> {
  match spans {
    Empty => []
    Cons { head: s, tail: rest } =>
      match s {
        Glyphs { glyphs } => Cons { head: glyphs, tail: glyph_spans_in(spans: rest) }
        Annotation { annotation: _ } => glyph_spans_in(spans: rest)
      }
  }
}

// The spans that carry the transcription. `LookedNothingReadable` yields NONE, which is how "looked, read no
// glyphs" became a fact of the type instead of a string a function has to recognise: rev 5 could only write
// it as "none" and then guess at it, which is the heuristic §4 says a closed system never needs.
fn glyph_spans(c: MarkingContent) -> List<GlyphSpan> {
  glyph_spans_in(spans: content_spans(c))
}

fn read_no_glyphs(c: MarkingContent) -> Bool {
  is_empty(xs: glyph_spans(c))
}

fn any_glyph_unread(spans: List<GlyphSpan>) -> Bool {
  match scalar_of(one: "?") {
    // Losing the mark's scalar costs the reading its SILENCE EXEMPTION rather than granting it: the reading
    // is then compared like any other, so a mint can fail but cannot be won by an unrun comparison. The
    // direction is deliberate, and is the one reduced_marking already takes when a separator has no scalar.
    Absent => false
    Present { value: mark } => any_span_carries_mark(spans: spans, mark: mark)
  }
}

// The unread mark is compared as a CHARACTER, obtained once from the one-character string the contract
// defines -- the way reduced_marking obtains "/" and " ". It is never compared as an alphabet. std's
// string_any_char_in is `!(for_all(xs: s, predicate: fn(c) { !(string_contains_char(s: alphabet, c: c)) }))`,
// and that predicate CAPTURES `alphabet`: its first labelled argument is the outer call's SECOND parameter.
// Handing that call an identifier in the `s:` position puts the identifier's own spelling into the nested
// call's parameter slot, and nothing says so until execution deep inside the capture -- four tests of the
// marking witness died with "calling 'unicode_scalar_unfold': no parameter named 'glyphs' (declared: [s])"
// after tokenize, parse, resolve and typecheck had all passed. A literal there is fine, and so is a call:
// only an identifier leaks, which is why each of the two shapes that looked like the trigger was green on
// its own. string_contains_char passes `xs: s` -- the first parameter, no capture -- and the same bound
// identifier through it ran green (variant f2, kept with this work's receipts in the private atlas branch).
// So the mark is a Char here, obtained once, and the test is membership.
// The leaf is its own function on purpose, and this split is load-bearing rather than tidiness. The label leak
// described above needs BOTH halves in one function: f8 (recursive arm + bound coercion + the capturing
// predicate) is red while f2 (the same binding in a function that does not recurse) and f9 (the same arm
// without the recursive tail) are both green. So the coercion is bound in a function that never calls itself,
// and the walk below binds nothing at all.
fn span_carries_mark(s: GlyphSpan, mark: Char) -> Bool {
  string_contains_char(s: s.span as String, c: mark)
}

fn any_span_carries_mark(spans: List<GlyphSpan>, mark: Char) -> Bool {
  match spans {
    Empty => false
    Cons { head: s, tail: rest } => span_carries_mark(s: s, mark: mark) || any_span_carries_mark(spans: rest, mark: mark)
  }
}

// THE SILENCE RULE, read off the type (contract §5, closed at rev 6). A reading dissents with nothing when
// it read no glyphs at all, or when a glyph it transcribed carries an unread mark.
//
// WHY '?' IS SILENCE AND NOT DISSENT, from the corpus rather than from argument: U12 reads
// `P617A / 01 14 / 226` while U13 — the same part type — reads `P617A / ?? ?? / ???`; Q3_5 carries `BC4X`
// beside `BC4X?`; `WEJ` beside `?WEJ` beside `?W?J`; `WG3` beside `?WG3`. Treating the mark as disagreement
// would invent conflicts between identical parts, and one such pair sits under an identification that relies
// on its reading today. '?' still records WHERE the read came from: a boxed glyph span containing '?' grounds
// provenance while staying silent about identity.
fn consumes_no_dispute(c: MarkingContent) -> Bool {
  read_no_glyphs(c) || any_glyph_unread(spans: glyph_spans(c))
}

// THE ONE REDUCTION, applied across a reading's glyph spans in the order they were written. An unreadable
// reduction stays a dispute rather than becoming a shrug, as §3 requires: no comparison ran, and reporting
// nothing would let `no_dissent` mint over a comparison that never happened.
fn reduce_glyphs(spans: List<GlyphSpan>) -> GlyphReduction {
  match spans {
    Empty => Reduced { lines: [] }
    Cons { head: s, tail: rest } =>
      match reduced_marking(text: s.span) {
        ReductionUnavailable { why } => ReductionUnavailable { why: why }
        Reduced { lines: here } =>
          match reduce_glyphs(spans: rest) {
            ReductionUnavailable { why } => ReductionUnavailable { why: why }
            Reduced { lines: further } => Reduced { lines: here + further }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn reduced_content(c: MarkingContent) -> GlyphReduction {
  reduce_glyphs(spans: glyph_spans(c))
}

fn line_disputes(
  relied_lines: List<ReducedLine>,
  other_lines: List<ReducedLine>
) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match relied_lines {
    Empty => other_lines.map(l => disputed_pair(left: missing_text(), right: text_of(glyphs: l.glyphs)))
    Cons { head: relied_line, tail: relied_rest } =>
      match other_lines {
        Empty =>
          relied_lines.map(l => disputed_pair(left: text_of(glyphs: l.glyphs), right: missing_text()))
        Cons { head: other_line, tail: other_rest } =>
          positions_in_dispute(
            relied_glyphs: relied_line.glyphs,
            other_glyphs: other_line.glyphs
          )
            + line_disputes(relied_lines: relied_rest, other_lines: other_rest)
      }
  }
}

fn disputed_glyphs(relied: MarkingReading, others: List<MarkingReading>) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  fold_list(
    xs: others,
    empty: [],
    cons: fn(acc, other) { acc + reading_disputes(relied: relied, other: other) }
  )
}

fn reading_disputes(relied: MarkingReading, other: MarkingReading) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  if consumes_no_dispute(c: relied.content) || consumes_no_dispute(c: other.content) {
    []
  } else {
    match reduced_content(c: relied.content) {
      ReductionUnavailable { why } => {
        let un = shown_text(t: why)
        [disputed_pair(left: "(reduction unavailable)", right: un)]
      }
      Reduced { lines: relied_lines } =>
        match reduced_content(c: other.content) {
          ReductionUnavailable { why } => {
            let un = shown_text(t: why)
            [
              disputed_pair(left: "(reduction unavailable)", right: un)
            ]
          }
          Reduced { lines: other_lines } =>
            line_disputes(relied_lines: relied_lines, other_lines: other_lines)
        }
    }
  }
}

// CONTROL 7, rev-6 form (§6.3): ONE OBSERVED SPAN, ONE CHANNEL. Among the spans that carry a sighting, two
// spans classified DIFFERENTLY and claiming the SAME digest and the SAME box is the contradiction — one thing
// observed, written once as glyphs and once as, say, a circumstance of the reading.
//
// WHY EXACT EVIDENCE IDENTITY AND NOTHING ELSE. The rev-5 qualification forbade inferring duplication "merely
// because the same characters appear in both fields", and a text test was struck on #15823 for the same
// reason; this asks only whether two spans claim one identical piece of evidence. `same_boxed_frame` is that
// question — cryptographic digest equality plus both regions present and all four edges equal. No text is
// compared, and boxes are never checked for OVERLAP: a part is shot at many zooms, so two overlapping boxes
// are two observations rather than one named twice. Spans without a sighting are outside the guarantee —
// recorded debt has no pixels to contradict — which is stated rather than hidden.
fn duplicate_channel_claim(r: MarkingReading) -> Bool {
  any_against(content_spans(r.content), content_spans(r.content))
}

// The pair scan, over the same list twice. Kept as its own function because this language has no `let`.
fn any_against(xs: List<MarkingSpan>, ys: List<MarkingSpan>) -> Bool {
  any(xs, a =>
    match span_sighting(s: a) {
      Absent => false
      Present { value: ca } =>
        any(ys, b =>
          channels_differ(a: a, b: b)
            && match span_sighting(s: b) {
                 Absent => false
                 Present { value: cb } => same_boxed_frame(a: ca, b: cb)
               }
        )
    }
  )
}

// Two contents carry the same glyphs when their reductions agree line for line. `same_reading` needs this and
// not string equality because the reduction says spaces inside a transcribed line are not glyph identity:
// "2BK G4" read from 05-06 and "2BK  G4" from 05-07 are one honest reading seen twice, not two readings.
fn same_glyph_text(a: MarkingContent, b: MarkingContent) -> Bool {
  match reduced_content(c: a) {
    // An unreducible reading is not the same reading as anything, including itself: identity here means a
    // comparison actually ran.
    ReductionUnavailable { why: _ } => false
    Reduced { lines: la } =>
      match reduced_content(c: b) {
        ReductionUnavailable { why: _ } => false
        Reduced { lines: lb } => same_reduced_lines(la: la, lb: lb)
      }
  }
}

// Char-level equality, so the language's own text equality never decides whether two readings agree (the
// rule it was written for: a comparison goes through Char, never through assembled text).
fn same_reduced_lines(la: List<ReducedLine>, lb: List<ReducedLine>) -> Bool {
  match la {
    Empty => is_empty(xs: lb)
    Cons { head: a, tail: a_rest } =>
      match lb {
        Empty => false
        Cons { head: b, tail: b_rest } =>
          same_chars(l: a.glyphs, r: b.glyphs) && same_reduced_lines(la: a_rest, lb: b_rest)
      }
  }
}

fn same_chars(l: List<Char>, r: List<Char>) -> Bool {
  match l {
    Empty => is_empty(xs: r)
    Cons { head: x, tail: xs } =>
      match r {
        Empty => false
        Cons { head: y, tail: ys } => char_eq(a: x, b: y) && same_chars(l: xs, r: ys)
      }
  }
}

// WHETHER TWO READINGS ARE THE ONE READING: the same glyphs, from the same evidence. This is what the
// contract's duplicate refusal compares, and it compares evidence as well as glyphs because two frames can
// carry the same honest reading — which is agreement, the thing `no_dissent` exists to record, and not a copy
// of one row into a list to give it weight.
fn same_reading(a: MarkingReading, b: MarkingReading) -> Bool {
  same_glyph_text(a: a.content, b: b.content) && same_evidence(a: a.evidence, b: b.evidence)
}

fn same_evidence(a: EvidenceRef, b: EvidenceRef) -> Bool {
  match a {
    OperatorStatement { stated_on: sa, verbatim: va } =>
      match b {
        OperatorStatement { stated_on: sb, verbatim: vb } => {
          let said_a = shown_text(t: sa)
          let said_b = shown_text(t: sb)
          let on_a = shown_text(t: va)
          let on_b = shown_text(t: vb)
          string_eq(a: said_a, b: said_b) && string_eq(a: on_a, b: on_b)
        }
        OperatorPhotograph { citation: _ } => false
        SellerListingPhotograph { listing: _, read_on: _ } => false
      }
    OperatorPhotograph { citation: ca } =>
      match b {
        OperatorPhotograph { citation: cb } => same_sighting(a: ca, b: cb)
        OperatorStatement { stated_on: _, verbatim: _ } => false
        SellerListingPhotograph { listing: _, read_on: _ } => false
      }
    SellerListingPhotograph { listing: la, read_on: ra } =>
      match b {
        SellerListingPhotograph { listing: lb, read_on: rb } => {
          let left = shown_text(t: la)
          let right = shown_text(t: lb)
          let from_a = shown_text(t: ra)
          let from_b = shown_text(t: rb)
          string_eq(a: left, b: right) && string_eq(a: from_a, b: from_b)
        }
        OperatorStatement { stated_on: _, verbatim: _ } => false
        OperatorPhotograph { citation: _ } => false
      }
  }
}

// A DUPLICATE READING IS REFUSED (contract §2): the same (evidence, text) pair may not appear twice, so a
// frame cannot be copied into `others` to give it weight. Reported rather than hidden, because the row is
// the thing at fault and a witness has to name which row.
fn has_duplicate_reading(relied: MarkingReading, others: List<MarkingReading>) -> Bool {
  any(others, o => same_reading(a: relied, b: o))
    || match first_duplicate_by_key(others, key: fn(r) { reading_key(r: r) }) {
         Present { value: _ } => true
         Absent => false
       }
}

// The key that notices a row wrote the same reading twice. It is a DIAGNOSTIC spelling -- the report's own
// words for the reading -- and it is used for nothing else. Where two readings must be judged the same
// SIGHTING, that goes through `same_reading` and the store's content identity, never through text.
fn reading_key(r: MarkingReading) -> String {
  match reduced_content(c: r.content) {
    // Distinct keys for distinct failures, so a row whose reduction cannot run never groups with a row whose
    // reduction produced text.
    ReductionUnavailable { why } => join(["(unreducible:", why as String, ") @ "], evidence_ref_text(e: r.evidence))
    Reduced { lines: ls } => join([lines_text(ls: ls), " @ "], evidence_ref_text(e: r.evidence))
  }
}

// The transcribed lines, reduced and rejoined with the separator, for KEYS ONLY. Comparison never goes
// through text — `same_chars` does that — but a key has to be a String to group by.
fn lines_text(ls: List<ReducedLine>) -> String {
  match ls {
    Empty => ""
    Cons { head: l, tail: rest } =>
      match rest {
        Empty => text_of(glyphs: l.glyphs)
        Cons { head: _, tail: _ } => join([text_of(glyphs: l.glyphs), "/", lines_text(ls: rest)], "")
      }
  }
}

// ─── THE RELIANCE AN OBSERVATION CARRIES (marking contract §2, revision 5) ──────────────────────
//
// WHY AN EXPLICIT STANDING RATHER THAN "MORE THAN ONE READING EXISTS". Because the second kind of free
// pass is a row that lists several readings and lets the earliest win. A standing records WHY one reading
// may carry an identification, and there are exactly three reasons the contract allows, each with its own
// sealed witness: the marking was read once, several readings agree, or the readings dissent and the
// bench preferred the one that is checkable.
//
// WHY THE WITNESSES ARE SEALED AND THE MINTS ARE PUBLIC. Only a constructor can decide an absence, and a
// row may not have a quantifier: `only_reading` is the only thing in the model that can produce an
// `OnlyReadingWitness`, so "no other reading of this marking exists" is decided by code and not written
// by an author. Sealing stops the forged standing -- an arm anyone can type is not a verdict -- while
// keeping the check reviewable, which is why these mints are ordinary checked functions rather than the
// `admit_callers` lists an earlier draft had: a gate naming its callers proves who called, not that the
// check passed, and it puts an authorisation list in the public graph for a fact the graph already shows.
// Each witness carries the SUBJECT it was minted for, so a witness minted for one specimen cannot be
// pasted into another specimen's row without a witness noticing.
//
// DIVERGENCE FROM THE CONTRACT'S WRITTEN SIGNATURES, stated per DESIGN §3b. The contract types
// `only_reading(roster, observation: MarkingObservation)`, but the observation CARRIES the reliance a mint
// produces -- a mint cannot consume the record it is being asked to complete. All three mints therefore
// consume a `MarkingReadingSet`: the same three fields, without the standing. The contract's own
// `prefer_checkable(subject, relied, dissents)` was already written that way, and its `dissents: Bool`
// parameter is dropped here for a reason §2b gives itself -- "neither can be written by hand by a row",
// and a Bool parameter is a row writing the dissent verdict. `MarkingReliance` stays an ordinary sum
// because a seal binds records and not coproduct arms (extdeps/external_authority, the precedent this
// follows, says so); what is sealed is each arm's witness, so the arm anyone can type carries nothing
// that decides anything.

// What a mint is asked about: the readings of one marking on one specimen, before any standing.
type MarkingReadingSet {
  subject: ComponentInstanceIdentity
  relied_on: MarkingReading
  others: List<MarkingReading>
}

type OnlyReadingWitness sole_constructor {
  subject: ComponentInstanceIdentity
}

type NoDissentWitness sole_constructor {
  subject: ComponentInstanceIdentity
}

// The one basis the contract allows. A fourth kind arrives with a consumer that reads it, not before.
type PreferenceKind = CheckablePreferredOverUncheckable { }

type ConflictSelectionBasis sole_constructor {
  subject: ComponentInstanceIdentity
  basis: PreferenceKind
}

type MarkingReliance
  = OnlyReading { witness: OnlyReadingWitness }
  | NoDissent { witness: NoDissentWitness }
  | PreferredUnderDissent { basis: ConflictSelectionBasis }

// The readings of one marking on one specimen, with the standing they may carry. The subject is a field
// rather than an assumption: a reading is checkable as a reading OF something, and a row that cannot name
// whose marking it read has no subject to bind its evidence to.
type MarkingObservation {
  subject: ComponentInstanceIdentity
  relied_on: MarkingReading
  others: List<MarkingReading>
  reliance: MarkingReliance
}

// Whether a reading is CHECKABLE AS A READING OF THIS SUBJECT: the board's own roster attributes this exact
// citation -- these bytes AND these pixels -- to this specimen. Not "the store has these bytes": an
// overview frame carries dozens of components, so bytes that exist say nothing about whose marking they
// show, and contract §2b rules that digest alone does not bind. A citation with no region fails here even
// when its bytes are in the store: fail closed, because the alternative is letting unboxed pixels into
// reliance, which is what §2b refuses outright.
fn reading_checkable_for_subject(
  roster: BoardSightings,
  subject: ComponentInstanceIdentity,
  reading: MarkingReading
) -> Bool {
  match evidence_citation(e: reading.evidence) {
    Present { value: citation } => sighting_attributed(roster: roster, subject: subject, citation: citation)
    // A spoken statement or a seller's listing image is evidence of a kind, and is not bytes the store can
    // check. It may sit in `others`; it cannot carry an identification.
    Absent => false
  }
}

// What no row may get wrong before a standing is even considered: no reading written twice, and no
// observed span recorded in both channels. Both are faults of the ROW rather than of the world, so every
// mint refuses them; a standing minted over a row that duplicates its own evidence is a standing over
// padding.
// §6.3, ENFORCED WHERE RELIANCE IS BORN. A reading may CARRY an identification only when:
//   * at least one glyph was actually read — `LookedNothingReadable` describes a body that was looked at and
//     not read, and rev 6's whole point is that this is now visible in the type instead of hiding in the
//     string "none". It stays a legal row, and it may sit among `others`; it cannot be relied on. Without
//     this clause rev 6 would have quietly weakened rev 5, whose entire `MarkingGlyphs` type existed to make
//     a no-glyph row unwriteable.
//   * EVERY glyph span that contributes to the transcription carries its own sighting, and that sighting is
//     attributed to THIS subject by the board's roster. A span whose sighting is Absent is recorded evidence
//     debt — legal to record, legal among `others`, and unable to carry weight. Digest alone never binds: an
//     overview frame shows dozens of components, so bytes that exist say nothing about whose marking they
//     show (§2b).
//
// WHY HERE AND NOT IN A CENSUS. The mint is where reliance comes from, so refusing there makes the illegal
// state unconstructible; a counting process that reports unboxed relied-on readings instead would be a
// census instrument, which §6 retires. Coverage is reported once, from the derivation script, as a number.
fn reliance_may_rest_on(
  roster: BoardSightings,
  subject: ComponentInstanceIdentity,
  reading: MarkingReading
) -> Bool {
  reading_checkable_for_subject(roster: roster, subject: subject, reading: reading)
    && !read_no_glyphs(reading.content)
    && every_glyph_bound(spans: glyph_spans(reading.content), roster: roster, subject: subject)
}

fn every_glyph_bound(
  spans: List<GlyphSpan>,
  roster: BoardSightings,
  subject: ComponentInstanceIdentity
) -> Bool {
  match spans {
    Empty => true
    Cons { head: s, tail: rest } =>
      match s.sighting {
        // Recorded debt, not a lie about pixels: it may be read, and it may not be relied on.
        Absent { } => false
        Present { value: citation } =>
          sighting_attributed(roster: roster, subject: subject, citation: citation)
            && every_glyph_bound(spans: rest, roster: roster, subject: subject)
      }
  }
}

fn reading_set_is_wellformed(readings: MarkingReadingSet) -> Bool {
  !has_duplicate_reading(relied: readings.relied_on, others: readings.others)
    && !duplicate_channel_claim(r: readings.relied_on)
    && !any(readings.others, o => duplicate_channel_claim(r: o))
}

// ONLY READING: the relied-on reading is checkable for this subject, and no second reading of the same
// marking exists. A row cannot assert that absence; this function is the only thing in the model that can
// produce the witness, and it produces it only over a roster that ties these pixels to this specimen.
fn only_reading(roster: BoardSightings, readings: MarkingReadingSet) -> MarkingReliance? {
  if !reading_set_is_wellformed(readings: readings) {
    Absent
  } else if !is_empty(xs: readings.others) {
    Absent
  } else if !reliance_may_rest_on(
    roster: roster, subject: readings.subject, reading: readings.relied_on
  ) {
    Absent
  } else {
    Present { value: OnlyReading { witness: OnlyReadingWitness { subject: readings.subject } } }
  }
}

// NO DISSENT: the readings agree under the one reduction. It requires at least one other reading -- an
// agreement with nobody is the free pass this standing exists to refuse, and `only_reading` is the
// standing for a single reading. Stricter than the contract's wording, which names only the refusal when
// readings dissent; stated here so the difference is on the record rather than in a reviewer's surprise.
fn no_dissent(roster: BoardSightings, readings: MarkingReadingSet) -> MarkingReliance? {
  if !reading_set_is_wellformed(readings: readings) {
    Absent
  } else if is_empty(xs: readings.others) {
    Absent
  } else if !reliance_may_rest_on(
    roster: roster, subject: readings.subject, reading: readings.relied_on
  ) {
    Absent
  } else if !is_empty(xs: disputed_glyphs(relied: readings.relied_on, others: readings.others)) {
    Absent
  } else {
    Present { value: NoDissent { witness: NoDissentWitness { subject: readings.subject } } }
  }
}

// PREFERRED UNDER DISSENT: the readings do dissent, the relied-on reading is checkable AS A READING OF
// THIS SUBJECT, and at least one dissenting reading is not. The contract's `dissents: Bool` is computed
// here instead, from `disputed_glyphs`, because §2b forbids a row writing its own dissent verdict -- and
// `CheckablePreferredOverUncheckable` would otherwise be a label an author could put on any preference
// whatsoever, which is the "row chooses" route this whole section exists to close.
fn prefer_checkable(roster: BoardSightings, readings: MarkingReadingSet) -> MarkingReliance? {
  let disputes = disputed_glyphs(relied: readings.relied_on, others: readings.others)
  if !reading_set_is_wellformed(readings: readings) {
    Absent
  } else if is_empty(xs: disputes) {
    Absent
  } else if !reliance_may_rest_on(
    roster: roster, subject: readings.subject, reading: readings.relied_on
  ) {
    Absent
  } else if !any(readings.others, o =>
    !reading_checkable_for_subject(roster: roster, subject: readings.subject, reading: o)) {
    Absent
  } else {
    Present {
      value: PreferredUnderDissent {
        basis: ConflictSelectionBasis {
          subject: readings.subject,
          basis: CheckablePreferredOverUncheckable { }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// Whether an observation's own witness was minted for its own subject. The seal stops a stranger minting
// a standing; this catches the subtler paste -- a witness genuinely minted for D2, carried into U49's row,
// where every field still type-checks. It is a required row of the witness, because the mints cannot see
// the observation they were not given.
fn reliance_matches_subject(o: MarkingObservation) -> Bool {
  match o.reliance {
    OnlyReading { witness } => component_identity_eq(a: witness.subject, b: o.subject)
    NoDissent { witness } => component_identity_eq(a: witness.subject, b: o.subject)
    PreferredUnderDissent { basis } => component_identity_eq(a: basis.subject, b: o.subject)
  }
}


type PrintedRating = PrintedFuseRating { current: Ampere, interrupt_voltage: Volt? }

// TWO WAYS A FOOTPRINT SAYS SOMETHING ABOUT WHAT IS IN IT, read off a photograph rather than off a
// marking. FootprintUnpopulated is absence: the pads are bare in a frame that identifies itself by
// content, and it means no part is fitted there on this board. Its twin, BodySeenMarkingUnread, is
// presence read the same way — a body stands in the cited region of the cited frame and no marking was
// read from it — and it makes no claim about what the part is. The pair stays distinguishable in the
// type. Do not write a sighted body as MarkingUnresolved with no text: that arm means a part named in
// the session that nobody saw, and its observed_presence is PresenceUnobserved, so folding "we saw a
// body" into it would make "we saw a part" and "we never looked" one state.
type ComponentIdentification
  = IdentifiedByMarking { row: ElectronicComponentRow, marking: MarkingReading }
  | MarkingSharedByMakers { rows: List<ElectronicComponentRow>, marking: MarkingReading }
  | MarkingCandidate { row: ElectronicComponentRow, marking: MarkingReading, discriminator: NonEmptyStr }
  | RatingPrintedOnBody { rating: PrintedRating, marking: MarkingReading }
  | ResistanceCodeRead { milliohms: Nat, marking: MarkingReading }
  | MarkingUnresolved { marking: MarkingReading?, discriminator: NonEmptyStr }
  | FootprintUnpopulated { evidence: EvidenceRef }
  | BodySeenMarkingUnread { evidence: EvidenceRef }

// WHEN THE COMPONENT WAS SEEN. seen is the observation's order against the board's dividing events, in
// the vocabulary its readings use (product.electrical.continuity ReadingOrder). A part removed from the
// board later is still the part that was seen fitted before, and only an order stated against that
// removal says which side of it the observation fell on (product.electrical.bench_session
// place_against). Whether a part was there at all is what the observation saw (observed_presence), and
// whether it has been removed is the board's alteration ledger: neither is authored here.
type InstalledComponent {
  component: ComponentInstanceIdentity
  terminals: List<TerminalIdentity>
  identification: ComponentIdentification
  seen: ReadingOrder
}

// WHAT THE BENCH ESTABLISHED ABOUT A BODY, APART FROM WHAT THE PART IS. A specimen's terminals are
// positions on a physical body, so they come from the body: either the positions a probe touched, or
// the leads counted around the pin-1 mark. They NEVER come from the numbering of a part the row is
// identified as or a candidate for: a datasheet table says how a maker numbers its leads, which is a
// fact about the PART and says nothing about how many leads this body has or which ones were reached
// (DESIGN §4d). Before this carrier existed, that line held only in prose, so a predicate could not
// check it; the guard is product.p4261.carrier_layout_witness's
// a_specimens_body_is_what_was_probed_and_not_a_named_parts_numbering.
//
// A count and a probe set are different observations, so they are different rows: a part may have all
// its leads counted and only two of them probed. Where a part has both, the probed positions are
// positions ON the counted body, so the probe set is a subset of the counted positions -- a body whose
// probes fall outside its own lead count is a contradiction, not a wider sample. Rows live on the
// board that observed them, in the same pattern as product.p4261.e1_body_sightings: one row per
// observation, each citing the frame or statement it was read from.
type ObservedLeadCount {
  component: ComponentInstanceIdentity
  count: Nat
  counted_from: NonEmptyStr
  evidence: EvidenceRef
}

// One position of one body that a probe touched, named as the bench counted it (the pin-1 mark fixes
// the direction, and the numbering is the body's, not a maker's). `evidence` is the frame or statement
// that establishes the probe: a position nobody reached is not an observation.
type ObservedProbedPosition {
  component: ComponentInstanceIdentity
  position: NonEmptyStr
  evidence: EvidenceRef
}

// WHAT AN OBSERVATION ESTABLISHES ABOUT PRESENCE. A part read in any way -- a matched, shared,
// candidate or unresolved marking, a printed rating, a resistance code -- was seen on the board; an
// empty footprint was seen without one. An entry with no marking read and no other reading records a
// part named in the session, not one seen: its presence is unobserved, and the entry's type name is
// not evidence.
type ObservedPresence
  = PartPresent
  | PartAbsent
  | PresenceUnobserved

fn observed_presence(i: ComponentIdentification) -> ObservedPresence {
  match i {
    IdentifiedByMarking { row: _, marking: _ } => PartPresent
    MarkingSharedByMakers { rows: _, marking: _ } => PartPresent
    MarkingCandidate { row: _, marking: _, discriminator: _ } => PartPresent
    RatingPrintedOnBody { rating: _, marking: _ } => PartPresent
    ResistanceCodeRead { milliohms: _, marking: _ } => PartPresent
    MarkingUnresolved { marking, discriminator: _ } => match marking { Present { value: _ } => PartPresent, Absent => PresenceUnobserved }
    FootprintUnpopulated { evidence: _ } => PartAbsent
    BodySeenMarkingUnread { evidence: _ } => PartPresent
  }
}

// An observation whose order against the board's dividing events was not stated: it is placed against
// none of them, so it can never stand for a state that follows a removal of its own part.
fn seen_unordered() -> ReadingOrder {
  OrderUnrecorded { relative_to: [] }
}

fn names(ns: List<NonEmptyStr>) -> List<TerminalIdentity> {
  map(ns, n => n as TerminalIdentity)
}

fn withstand_kind_key(k: VoltageWithstandKind) -> Nat {
  match k {
    TvsReverseStandoff => 0
    CapacitorRatedVoltage => 1
    DrainSourceMaximum => 2
    FuseInterruptRating => 3
    RectifierRepetitivePeak => 4
  }
}

fn withstand_list(rows: List<ElectronicComponentRow>) -> List<VoltageWithstand> {
  flat_map(rows, r => match voltage_withstand(spec: r.specification) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: w } => [w]
  })
}

fn highest_withstand(ws: List<VoltageWithstand>, w0: VoltageWithstand) -> VoltageWithstand {
  fold(ws, init: w0, f: (acc, w) => if volt_count(v: w.maximum) > volt_count(v: acc.maximum) { w } else { acc })
}

// THE PROPERTY EVERY CANDIDATE SUPPORTS. The installed part is one of the candidates, so a voltage limit
// holds for it only if it holds for all of them: they must agree on the kind of limit, and the limit
// carried is the HIGHEST candidate figure -- the rail stays below the installed part's figure, which is
// at most that. (The first revision carried the lowest, which a higher-rated installed candidate would
// falsify.) Candidates of different kinds support no limit.
fn agreed_withstand(rows: List<ElectronicComponentRow>, ws: List<VoltageWithstand>, w0: VoltageWithstand) -> List<VoltageWithstand> {
  if count(ws) == count(rows) && all(ws, w => withstand_kind_key(k: w.kind) == withstand_kind_key(k: w0.kind)) { [highest_withstand(ws: ws, w0: w0)] } else { [] }
}

fn shared_withstand(rows: List<ElectronicComponentRow>) -> VoltageWithstand? {
  let ws = withstand_list(rows: rows)
  match ws.first() {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: w0 } => agreed_withstand(rows: rows, ws: ws, w0: w0).first()
  }
}

fn identified_withstand(i: ComponentIdentification) -> VoltageWithstand? {
  match i {
    IdentifiedByMarking { row, marking: _ } => voltage_withstand(spec: row.specification)
    MarkingSharedByMakers { rows, marking: _ } => shared_withstand(rows: rows)
    MarkingCandidate { row: _, marking: _, discriminator: _ } => Absent
    RatingPrintedOnBody { rating, marking: _ } => match rating {
      PrintedFuseRating { current: _, interrupt_voltage } => match interrupt_voltage {
        Absent => Absent
        Present { value: v } => Present { value: VoltageWithstand { kind: FuseInterruptRating, maximum: v } }
      }
    }
    ResistanceCodeRead { milliohms: _, marking: _ } => Absent
    MarkingUnresolved { marking: _, discriminator: _ } => Absent
    FootprintUnpopulated { evidence: _ } => Absent
    BodySeenMarkingUnread { evidence: _ } => Absent
  }
}

// WHETHER AN IDENTIFICATION ESTABLISHES WHAT THE PART IS, decided on its arm and never on its text. A
// candidate stays a candidate however well it reads: its text exists to say what would settle it, and
// a finding that asked "is there text?" took that explanation for the identification (the
// candidate-as-identified defect). A marking several makers share names the part's rows and leaves only
// the maker open, so it identifies the part. A printed rating or a resistor code is the value the
// part carries, read off its body. An unresolved marking, a bare footprint and a body sighted without a
// readable marking identify nothing.
fn identification_established(i: ComponentIdentification) -> Bool {
  match i {
    IdentifiedByMarking { row: _, marking: _ } => true
    MarkingSharedByMakers { rows: _, marking: _ } => true
    MarkingCandidate { row: _, marking: _, discriminator: _ } => false
    RatingPrintedOnBody { rating: _, marking: _ } => true
    ResistanceCodeRead { milliohms: _, marking: _ } => true
    MarkingUnresolved { marking: _, discriminator: _ } => false
    FootprintUnpopulated { evidence: _ } => false
    BodySeenMarkingUnread { evidence: _ } => false
  }
}

// A converter pin's terminal is named from its row, so a reading and the row join on one spelling.
fn pin_terminal_name(number: Nat, name: NonEmptyStr) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["pin ", to_string(number), " (", name as String, ")"], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn regulator_pins(row: ElectronicComponentRow) -> List<RegulatorPin> {
  match row.specification {
    SwitchingRegulatorSpec { regulator } => regulator.pins
    _ => []
  }
}

fn no_pin_numbers() -> List<Nat> {
  []
}

// The numbered pins a manufacturer's pin table declares, whatever the part's kind; a part whose row
// carries no pin table answers none. A package feature with no pin number (an exposed pad) is not here.
fn numbered_pins(row: ElectronicComponentRow) -> List<Nat> {
  match row.specification {
    TvsDiodeSpec { tvs: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    RectifierSpec { rectifier: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    CapacitorSpec { capacitor: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    MosfetSpec { mosfet: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    TemperatureSensorSpec { sensor } => map(sensor.pins, p => p.number)
    ConnectorSpec { connector: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    HotSwapControllerSpec { controller } => map(controller.pins, p => p.number)
    ShiftRegisterSpec { register } => map(register.pins, p => p.number)
    LevelTranslatorSpec { translator } => map(translator.pins, p => p.number)
    LogicGateSpec { gate } => map(gate.pins, p => p.number)
    ProgrammableLogicSpec { logic: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    RootOfTrustSpec { root_of_trust: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    SerialFlashSpec { flash: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    SwitchingRegulatorSpec { regulator } => map(regulator.pins, p => p.number)
    BusMultiplexerSpec { multiplexer: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    UsbHubSpec { hub: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
    ClockFanoutSpec { clock: _ } => no_pin_numbers()
  }
}

// A pin read on a part whose readings name it by number alone.
fn pin_number_name(number: Nat) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["pin ", to_string(number)], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE BARE FOOTPRINTS ARE THEIR OWN POINTS. A pad read with its part lifted is a different physical
// state from the lead read with the part fitted, so each lifted converter's footprint is a component of
// its own (FootprintUnpopulated), its pads named from the row's pin order like the leads were, and every
// reading at it is ordered after the lift. Nothing in the record joins a pad to the lead that sat on it,
// so no netlist combines the intact board's readings with the bare footprint's by terminal identity,
// and a netlist reads one physical state of the board in any case (product.electrical.bench_session
// SpecimenState): the lift is a boundary, and the footprint's readings are of the state after it.
fn pad_terminal_name(number: Nat, name: NonEmptyStr) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["pad ", to_string(number), " (", name as String, ")"], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// Exact equality of two transcribed strings, character by character. This is the comparison the contract
// allows: it asks whether two RECORDED spans carry the same glyphs, so it is exact evidence comparison, not
// a matcher over prose. Nothing here splits, joins, normalises or searches -- a control that did would be a
// second, unaudited authority over what was printed (contract §1).
fn glyph_text_eq(a: MarkingGlyphs, b: MarkingGlyphs) -> Bool {
  let left = a as String
  let right = b as String
  same_chars(l: unicode_scalar_unfold(s: left), r: unicode_scalar_unfold(s: right))
}

// The reading's single span of GLYPHS, when the glyph channel holds exactly one. Spans in the other channels
// are not counted against it: letters plus the maker's logo is still one span of glyphs. Asking instead for
// "exactly one span of any kind" is the wrong question and was caught by the store witness -- it made the four
// FSV20100V rows look unread, because their ON logo sits in `rest` beside the glyphs. What must be unique is
// the glyph channel, since two spans of glyphs in one reading is the duplicate-channel claim that control 7
// refuses; where each span was sighted is a separate question, asked of each span where it matters.
fn sole_glyph_text(c: MarkingContent) -> MarkingGlyphs? {
  match glyph_spans_in(spans: content_spans(c)) {
    Empty => Absent
    Cons { head: only, tail: rest } =>
      match rest {
        Empty => Present { value: only.span }
        Cons { head: _, tail: _ } => Absent
      }
  }
}

// Exactly one annotation beside the transcription, and that annotation is a PrintedLogo. Each Coproduct arm
// is named rather than wildcarded, because this language takes no wildcard in a Coproduct match; the four
// arms also make it visible that only a logo qualifies.
fn one_printed_logo(beside: MarkingSpan, after: List<MarkingSpan>) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match beside {
    Glyphs { glyphs: _ } => Absent
    Annotation { annotation } =>
      match annotation {
        ReadingCircumstance { preceded_by: _, span: _, noted: _, sighting: _ } => Absent
        BodyFeature { preceded_by: _, span: _, described: _, sighting: _ } => Absent
        PrintedLogo { preceded_by: _, span: mark, whose: _, sighting: _ } =>
          match after {
            Empty => Present { value: mark }
            Cons { head: _, tail: _ } => Absent
          }
      }
  }
}

// HOW A READING READS IN A REPORT. VERBATIM: each recorded span's own text, with the annotations bracketed
// so a reader of a finding can see that an aside is not part of the marking. This deliberately does NOT go
// through the reduction -- the reduction declares spacing inside a line non-glyph for COMPARISON ("TI 31"
// and "TI31" are one reading), and a report that printed the comparison form would lose what the bench
// actually transcribed. Keys use the reduction (`reading_key`); prose like this never decides anything.
fn marking_text(r: MarkingReading) -> String {
  let glyphs = render_glyph_lines(gs: glyph_spans(r.content))
  let asides = render_asides(spans: content_spans(r.content))
  if is_empty(xs: glyph_spans(r.content)) {
    join(["no glyphs read ", asides], "")
  } else if string_eq(a: asides, b: "") {
    glyphs
  } else {
    join([glyphs, " ", asides], "")
  }
}

// The transcribed spans, in recorded order, joined by the line separator.
fn render_glyph_lines(gs: List<GlyphSpan>) -> String {
  match gs {
    Empty => ""
    Cons { head: g, tail: rest } =>
      match rest {
        Empty => g.span as String
        Cons { head: _, tail: _ } =>
          join([g.span as String, " / ", render_glyph_lines(gs: rest)], "")
      }
  }
}

// The non-glyph spans, bracketed. `Glyphs` arms are skipped: their text is already above, and printing one
// twice is how a single span ends up looking like two.
fn render_asides(spans: List<MarkingSpan>) -> String {
  match spans {
    Empty => ""
    Cons { head: s, tail: rest } =>
      match s {
        Glyphs { glyphs: _ } => render_asides(spans: rest)
        Annotation { annotation } =>
          match annotation {
            PrintedLogo { preceded_by: _, span, whose, sighting: _ } =>
              join(["(", span as String, " logo: ", whose as String, ") ", render_asides(spans: rest)], "")
            BodyFeature { preceded_by: _, span, described, sighting: _ } =>
              join(["(", span as String, ": ", described as String, ") ", render_asides(spans: rest)], "")
            ReadingCircumstance { preceded_by: _, span, noted, sighting: _ } =>
              join(["(", span as String, " -- ", noted as String, ") ", render_asides(spans: rest)], "")
          }
      }
  }
}

fn identification_text(i: ComponentIdentification) -> String? {
  match i {
    IdentifiedByMarking { row, marking } => Present { value: join([row.vendor.legal_name as String, " ", row.ordering_code as String, ", from the marking '", marking_text(marking), "'"], "") }
    MarkingSharedByMakers { rows, marking } => Present { value: join(["one of ", join(map(rows, r => join([r.vendor.legal_name as String, " ", r.ordering_code as String], "")), " or "), " (maker not identified), from the marking '", marking_text(marking), "'"], "") }
    MarkingCandidate { row, marking, discriminator } => Present { value: join(["CANDIDATE ", row.vendor.legal_name as String, " ", row.ordering_code as String, " from the marking '", marking_text(marking), "' (not identified: ", discriminator as String, ")"], "") }
    RatingPrintedOnBody { rating: _, marking } => Present { value: join(["rating printed on the body: '", marking_text(marking), "'"], "") }
    ResistanceCodeRead { milliohms, marking } => Present { value: join([to_string(milliohms), " milliohms by the resistor code '", marking_text(marking), "'"], "") }
    MarkingUnresolved { marking: _, discriminator: _ } => Absent
    FootprintUnpopulated { evidence: _ } => Absent
    BodySeenMarkingUnread { evidence: _ } => Absent
  }
}
